## Status

Context-mixed blocks still require causal model updates during decoding.
Earlier experiments on Silesia and enwik8 found zpaq `-m5` about 2–4% smaller.
Those corpora were outside the Bato 0.5 speed rerun.
# Bato format 1.3

A `.bato` stream holds file bytes or a serialized directory tree. Bato 0.6
accepts format 1.3 as its underlying compression stream.

The stream is a 10-byte header, zero or more blocks, and a footer. Fixed-width
integers are little-endian. Variable integers are canonical unsigned LEB128:
seven value bits per byte, low group first, high bit set when another byte
follows; redundant leading zero groups and values exceeding u64 are rejected.

## Header

| Offset | Bytes | Field |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | 4 | `BATO` |
| 4 | 1 | Major `1` |
| 5 | 1 | Minor `3` |
| 6 | 1 | Flags: bit 0 directory archive; other bits zero |
| 7 | 1 | Segment log2, 16..30 (default 26) |
| 8 | 1 | CM chunk log2, 12..20, no greater than segment log2 (default 18) |
| 9 | 1 | Model table-cap log2, 0..24 (default 22) |

The version identifies the entire format, model, and transforms. Geometry is
encoded once. Each segment is independent and has at most `2^segment_log2`
original bytes; only the final segment may be shorter.

## Blocks

The first byte packs type in bits 0..2 and transform in bits 3..5. Bits 6..7
must be zero, except the standalone end marker `255`. Original length `n`
follows as a variable integer. Types 1, 3, 4, 5 and 7 then encode payload length
`c` as a variable integer; it must be strictly smaller than `n`. The payload
is followed by four bytes of CRC32C over the original, untransformed bytes.
All lengths, segment boundaries and output limits are checked before payload
allocation. Blocks cannot cross segment boundaries.

| Type | Meaning | Payload |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | Raw CM chunk | Exactly `n` bytes; update history if a later CM coded chunk needs it |
| 1 | Coded CM chunk | `c` bytes of arithmetic code |
| 2 | Independent stored segment | Exactly `n` bytes, no model |
| 3 | Independent literal/copy segment | `c` bytes of commands |
| 4 | Independent static Huffman segment | `c` bytes of table and bits |
| 5 | Huffman-coded literal/copy segment | Token-stream length variable integer, then static table and bits |
| 6 | Repeated-byte segment | One byte repeated `n` times, `n >= 2` |
| 7 | Contextual digit-run segment | Literal skeleton and integer streams |

A segment holds either one independent block (2..7), or CM chunks (0..1).
All nonfinal CM chunks have the geometry's full chunk length. A CM segment
uses a single transform. Its model persists across chunks, including raw
chunks; decoders can skip updates for an all-raw segment or a raw suffix
after its final coded chunk. Models restart at segment boundaries.

The encoder compares complete block bytes, including size fields and CRCs.
It tries the untransformed independent representations and five reversible
filters. The existing x86 selector admits an x86 candidate. Context mixing
remains a candidate with its existing x86 decision. Early termination uses
only emitted coder bytes and mandatory framing as a lower bound, never a
sampled compression estimate. No filename, MIME routing, or quality modes
are involved. Encoder search is not normative; decoding semantics are.

### Literal/copy commands

A command begins with variable integer `v`. If even, `v >> 1` is a nonzero
literal length, followed by that many bytes. If odd, copy length is
`(v >> 1) + 4`, followed by a variable integer distance. Distance must be
nonzero and no greater than the already produced length. Overlapping
copies repeat already decoded bytes. Commands must produce exactly `n`
bytes with no trailing input. Decoder copies expand geometrically without
per-byte prediction or training.

### Static Huffman tables

The table starts with a variable integer symbol count, 2..256, then pairs
of symbol byte and code-length byte in strictly increasing symbol order.
Lengths are 1..15 and must form a complete prefix tree. Codes are canonical:
assign increasing codes by length then symbol. Their bits are emitted
most-significant code bit first into the least-significant available stream
bit. Unused high bits in the final byte are zero; trailing bytes and
nonzero padding are rejected. The original size determines decoded length.
For type 5 the prefixed length determines decoded token bytes, bounded by
`n + 10`, and the token decoder must then produce exactly `n` original bytes.
The decoder builds one lookup table; it does no adaptive training.

### Coded CM payload

The payload is a binary arithmetic code: 32-bit interval, 16-bit probabilities, byte renormalization and the shortest zero-padded final code point. The decoder checks the canonical prefix and the exact tail. Bit probabilities come from the context-mixing model in `src/core/cm`, which is the normative definition for this version:

* hashed order 0–4 and 6 contexts and four word/line contexts, each a nibble bit-history map in a shared cache-line table;
* an order-0 map, a byte-run model, a match model, a record-stride model and a bit-neighbourhood model;
* two auxiliary experts with their own mixers: text (orders 5 and 7, word + previous byte, three words) and sparse structure (bytes 2, 3, 4 back and the 2/3 pair);
* three first-layer mixers, a final mixer, three state-conditioned calibrators and three APMs.

All arithmetic is integer, so every platform produces identical bytes.

### Reversible transforms

| ID | Transform |
|---|---|
| 0 | None |
| 1 | x86 call/jump addresses |
| 2 | Byte delta at distance 1 |
| 3 | Byte delta at distance 2 |
| 4 | Byte delta at distance 4 |
| 5 | Byte-plane shuffle of 2-byte words |
| 6 | Byte-plane shuffle of 4-byte words |
| 7 | Reserved, rejected |

Delta writes `out[i] = input[i] - input[i-distance]` modulo 256, using zero
for positions before the start. Inverse adds restored preceding bytes.
Shuffle groups byte 0 from each full word, then byte 1, and so on; an
incomplete trailing word stays unchanged at the end. The inverse undoes
this permutation. Filters operate within a segment and CRCs are verified
after inversion.

When a segment's call/jump sites repeat absolute targets far more often than relative operands, the encoder rewrites the segment before modeling. Every chunk of that segment carries transform `1`; a segment mixing transform values is corrupt. The rewrite goes over positions `i` from `len − 5` down to `0`: if byte `i` is `0xE8` or `0xE9` and byte `i + 4` is `0x00` or `0xFF`, the 24-bit value in bytes `i+1..=i+3` becomes `(value + i) mod 2^24`, with `i` counted from the segment start. The decoder applies the inverse in ascending order, `(value − i) mod 2^24` with the same site test, which is exact for every input. Chunk CRCs cover the original bytes and are checked after the inverse.

## Footer

The standalone byte `255`, canonical variable integer total original length,
and four-byte CRC32C over the complete original output. Trailing bytes are
rejected. Empty streams are 16 bytes. A stored segment adds one tag byte,
one length variable integer and four CRC bytes; it has no payload-size field.

## Directory archives (flag bit 0)

The stream's decoded bytes are a catalogue of entries, each starting with a kind byte:

| kind | layout | meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `1` | path length (u16) · path | directory |
| `2` | path length (u16) · path · size (u64) · contents · CRC32C (u32) | file |
| `3` | path length (u16) · path · size (u64) · id (u32) · CRC32C (u32) | file identical to the earlier file entry number `id`, stored once |
| `0` | — | end of catalogue |

Paths are UTF-8, use `/` separators, and are relative. Absolute paths, `..`, empty components, duplicates and names that would alias on the target filesystem are rejected. Extraction writes into a staging directory, enforces entry, path, metadata and output limits, verifies every checksum, and publishes the tree only after everything is valid.

## Contextual digit-run representation (type 7)

This is a reversible byte transform, without a file-format parser. Maximal
ASCII decimal runs of 1..19 bytes become markers `0,1` in a literal skeleton.
Longer digit runs remain literal. Literal zero bytes are escaped as `0,0`.
The payload starts with variable integers for skeleton length, then one
skeleton representation type (2..6), its payload length, and that payload.
Nested type 7 is rejected. Skeleton output is bounded by twice original size.

The context is the low eight bits of FNV-1a over at most eight immediately
preceding original non-digit bytes, stopping at a digit or stream start.
The seed is 2166136261 and multiplier 16777619. A variable integer context
count (1..256) follows the skeleton. Entries occur in strictly increasing
context-byte order. Each entry has context byte, predictor byte, then three
bounded packets: low seven value bits, high value groups, leading-zero count.
A context's low and zero-count streams have one byte per numeric run. The
high stream is canonical ULEB128 for each value shifted right seven bits.

Predictor 0 sends the absolute unsigned integer. Predictor 1 subtracts the
last value of that context. Predictor 2 subtracts `2*last - previous`. All
operations wrap modulo 2^64. Modes 1/2 map the residual by signed zigzag
`(residual << 1) XOR (0 - (residual >> 63))`; mode 0 has no zigzag. Histories
start at zero. Decoding reverses that mapping, restores the predictor,
updates history, emits the indicated leading zeroes and canonical decimal
digits, and rejects a resulting run longer than 19 bytes. Every packet
must be consumed completely. Contexts are inferred from restored bytes.

A packet is original length, one codec byte, payload length, payload. Codec
0 is exact raw bytes; 1 repeats its one payload byte; 2 uses static rANS;
3 uses literal/copy commands; 4 prefixes the command-stream length and then
rANS-codes those commands. Command-stream output is bounded by packet length
plus ten. Low-stream counts sum to at most ceil(original length / 2), since digit
runs are maximal. High-stream output is bounded by nine bytes per number
and, across contexts, twice the original length plus two bytes. All lengths are
checked before allocation, and outer block/output limits and CRCs still apply.

Static rANS uses scale 12, total frequency 4096, lower state 2^23 and byte
renormalization. Its table is a variable integer symbol count (1..256), then
ascending symbol byte and variable integer frequency-minus-one. Frequencies
must sum exactly to 4096. The table is followed by a four-byte little-endian
state in [2^23,2^31), then renormalization bytes. For slot `state & 4095`,
the inverse update is `frequency * (state >> 12) + slot - cumulative`.
Read bytes while the resulting state is below 2^23. The final state must be
2^23 with no remaining payload. Frequencies are transmitted, so the decoder
does not train a model. Encoder normalization and predictor choices are not
part of the decoding contract. All choices compare complete serialized bytes.

## Archive envelopes and split volumes

Bato 0.6 adds separately versioned wrappers for directory archives. Plain
`BATO` streams keep format 1.3 and the same encoded bytes. All wrapper
integers are unsigned and little-endian.

- `BAE1` is followed by one binary [age v1](https://c2sp.org/age) passphrase
  payload containing the complete compressed Bato directory stream.
- `BAP1` is followed by a u64 public stream length, the public Bato directory
  stream, and one binary age v1 passphrase payload. The age plaintext contains
  the 32-byte SHA-256 of the compressed public stream, a u16 protection scope
  count, the scopes, and the private Bato directory stream. Each scope is a
  u16 byte length and that many UTF-8 bytes in catalogue path syntax. A scope
  includes the exact path and its descendants. The sections may share ancestor
  directories. Full extraction authenticates the private stream and checks its
  binding to the public stream before publishing files. Public-only extraction
  verifies only the public Bato stream and any split-volume checksum.
- A split archive is a directory with an `index.bato` file and the volume
  directory named by that index. The index contains `BAV1`, a u64 maximum part
  size, a u64 total archive size, the 32-byte SHA-256 of the complete archive, and
  a 12-byte ASCII alphanumeric suffix. Volume files are named `part-0000001`
  onward in `volumes-<suffix>`. Each part is the maximum size except the last
  when there is a remainder. The assembled bytes form a plain or encrypted
  directory archive as described above. Updates publish a new index only after
  the complete replacement generation is written.

Password envelopes use age's scrypt recipient with `N=2^17, r=8, p=1`.
Encryption randomness is independent of Bato's deterministic compressor.

## Checkpoints and recovery

Bato 0.7 keeps the normal `BATO` 1.3 stream. A local `BAJ2` checkpoint
records complete segment boundaries, accumulated CRC32C, and SHA-256
identities for the source and staged output. Private checkpoint records use
independent age X25519 payloads. The job identity is stored in an age
passphrase envelope. These files are local job state and are separate from
the finished archive.

The optional `BAV2` split index uses the same fields as `BAV1`, followed
by one 32-byte SHA-256 per numbered volume and a final SHA-256 for the
`parity` file. The parity file is the bytewise XOR of all numbered volumes,
with the shorter final data volume treated as zero-padded. The parity file
length is the longest data volume length. The combined archive SHA-256
must verify after reconstruction.

A `BAR1` salvage index is generated by fully decoding and verifying an
intact plain stream. It stores the original stream header, encoded and
decoded segment lengths, per-segment CRC32C and SHA-256, and directory
member paths and raw extents. A SHA-256 footer protects the index against
accidental damage. Salvage validates each segment independently and uses
the directory catalogue checks when restoring complete files.

`BAV3` extends the split manifest with a parity count byte after the
generation suffix, followed by the data-volume SHA-256 values and the
parity-volume SHA-256 values. The parity files are named `parity-01` and
onward. They are Reed-Solomon parity over GF(2⁸), with short data volumes
zero-padded for coding. Data and parity use the systematic matrix of
`reed-solomon-erasure` 6. Reads verify the assembled archive hash.

`BAR2` extends `BAR1` with a 32-byte SHA-256 of the complete source archive
immediately after its magic. Selective indexed extraction requires this
binding. Salvage accepts both versions and can operate on a damaged source.

### Embedded indexes

`BAI1` contains a little-endian u64 BAR2 length, the complete BAR2 index, and
the original directory stream to EOF. The BAR2 index binds the stream's
SHA-256 and independently checks decoded segment SHA-256 values. Its size
limit is 64 MiB.

`BAE2` contains a single password-encrypted age v1 file to EOF. Its plaintext
is the same body as BAI1, starting with the length. The age stream
authenticates its header, encrypted chunks and final length. The reader seeks
within the authenticated plaintext to reach selected Bato segments and
verifies their decoded SHA-256 values from the encrypted BAR2 index. A
selection verifies the index, final age chunk and chunks it reads. Full
verification reads the entire stream and checks the BAR2 stream binding.
# Reproducible Bato improvements

Keep downloaded corpora, frozen manifests, benchmark databases, binaries,
profiles, generated reports, and checkpoints in ignored directories such as
`multi_corpus/` and `artifacts/`. The authoritative tools are
`scripts/download_corpus.py`, `scripts/benchmark_suite.py`, and
`scripts/compare_benchmarks.py`.

The SQLite store uses full-synchronous rollback journaling under the existing
host measurement lock. Before marking a run complete it checks database
integrity, the planned case count, and every accepted fresh case's committed
warmup and measured trial numbers. Preserve a damaged database and journals;
start fresh measurements in a new ignored output directory rather than
silently trusting an inconsistent projection.
The comparison reader independently checks database integrity, accepted trial
numbers, and the case projection against the committed trials. Schema-v4 JSON
reports alone cannot establish a comparison: preserve the canonical database.
If the environment replaces or damages a live database, retain the failure
and use an unsynchronized temporary measurement directory, then back up the
completed database and reports into an ignored directory. Do not repair lost
observations by copying values from a report.

## Freeze before evaluating

Download and validate real data with the downloader. For example:

```bash
python3 scripts/download_corpus.py -o multi_corpus --target compression
mkdir -p artifacts/iteration
python3 scripts/benchmark_suite.py -c multi_corpus --mode rigorous --target canterbury \
  --track both --workload both --list-workloads > artifacts/iteration/development.frozen.json
python3 scripts/benchmark_suite.py -c multi_corpus --mode rigorous --target calgary \
  --track both --workload both --list-workloads > artifacts/iteration/heldout.frozen.json
```

Declare the dataset split, manifest hashes, hardware, binary source revision,
profile, CPU allocation, thread limits, memory limits, group size, seed,
warmup/trial counts, timeout, and uncertainty rule in an ignored checkpoint.
Tune only on development data. Evaluate held-out data after selecting each
candidate; do not use its results to choose parser parameters. Synthetic
fixtures are useful for corruption, bounds, and parity testing, but do not
replace real-data measurements.

For every registered asset, including the large tier, use the actual downloader
with an explicit prepared-data budget, then audit the indexed files:

```bash
python3 scripts/download_corpus.py -o multi_corpus --all --tier extreme \
  --jobs 8 --per-host 2 --extraction-jobs 2 --max-bytes 10000000000
python3 scripts/download_corpus.py -o multi_corpus --audit-existing
python3 scripts/benchmark_suite.py -c multi_corpus --mode rigorous --suite corpus \
  --workload directories --track both --include-duplicates \
  --max-file-bytes 8589934592 --max-directory-bytes 8589934592 \
  --list-workloads > artifacts/iteration/full-collections.frozen.json
```

Whole collection directory workloads cover every indexed byte without
sampling. Verify that their file paths and hashes exactly equal the index;
record this scope distinctly from benchmarking every individual file. Keep
duplicate files in the complete directory trees. Freeze the collection
manifest and the development/held-out split before measurements.

## Measure separately for each configuration

Use `--check-tools` to preflight the actual installed tools and record
versions. Missing RAR, KGB, or any other adapter must remain explicitly missing.
The `high` profile applies 7z `-mx=9`, ZIP `-9`, RAR `-m5`, KGB `-9`,
xz `-9 --extreme --no-adjust`, zstd `--ultra -22`, gzip `-9`, LZ4 `-12`,
and bzip2 `-9`. These are tool settings, not evidence that a competitor's
size is optimal for every input. Fast and default settings remain available
as separate profiles; never compare unlike profiles silently.

Example for one CPU and all available competitors at their highest settings:

```bash
python3 scripts/benchmark_suite.py -c multi_corpus --target canterbury \
  --mode rigorous --track both --workload both \
  --frozen-workloads artifacts/iteration/development.frozen.json \
  --bato-bin artifacts/iteration/baseline --profile high \
  --cpu-limit 1 --threads 1 --bato-modes cpu1 \
  --bato-memory-budget 1073741824 \
  --memory-budget 2147483648 --warmups 1 --repetitions 5 \
  --timeout 120 --allow-unavailable --label baseline-cpu1
```

Save the printed run ID and binary hash. Repeat the exact settings with a
candidate binary. Change `--cpu-limit`, `--threads`, and `--bato-modes`
together for an equal-resource parallel track, e.g. eight allocated CPUs and
`--bato-modes cpu`. Serial competitors remain serial within the same
allocation; do not pretend their CLI supports thread settings it lacks.
Bato has no GPU path. A GPU competitor (`--nncp-cuda`) is a separate
hardware track and is never compared with CPU runs as if equal.

Pass both `--large-warmups 1 --large-repetitions 5` as well as
`--warmups 1 --repetitions 5` for the full suite. Large workloads must not
silently use a single trial. The conservative scratch admission reserves five
times the payload and per-file headers plus 64 MiB; free reproducible build and
download temporaries after pinning binaries if necessary. Do not lower the
admission factor to make a large workload pass. Resource failures and timeouts
remain explicit results, never competitor wins. A Bato-only full-data run
can validate engine changes, but does not replace the full competitor matrix.

Native directory archives and a normalized common-tar input are separate
tracks. External compression and tar packaging occur only in Python benchmark
tooling. Every decompressed file, path, and directory, including empty
directories, is verified before a trial is accepted. Common-tar trials verify
the exact restored tar bytes, then safely extract that tar and compare every
file and directory with the frozen source snapshot. These verification checks
occur outside codec compression/decompression timing. Compression and
decompression wall time, throughput, and isolated workflow CPU time have
separate recorded scopes. Container construction is included in native
workflow timing; common-tar construction is prepared outside encoder timing.

## Compare and retain useful changes

```bash
python3 scripts/compare_benchmarks.py --baseline BASELINE_RUN_ID \
  --candidate CANDIDATE_RUN_ID --competitors bato-v1
```

The output contains per-case comparisons and exclusion reasons. Exact stable
bytes determine size wins. Timed wins or losses require a warmup, at least
five fresh trials, a difference greater than 3%, and disjoint observed trial
ranges. A 1 ms wall-time floor avoids claiming sub-millisecond differences
as wins; CPU time has a 0.1 ms floor. Overlapping ranges are uncertain, not
statistical confidence intervals. Small changes inside the tolerance are
reported as ties. Run-to-run drift and the sequential evaluation order remain
limitations even with this conservative rule.

Use `--competitor-run PEER_RUN_ID --competitors bato-v1` (or one explicit
parallel configuration) to reference existing competitor trials. The command
requires identical hardware/harness identity, CPU allocation, requested threads,
memory policy, profile, seed, timeout, and evaluation mode. It reads each peer's
original committed trials and records both source run IDs. It does not copy
observations into a new run. Failed, cached, short, missing, or incompatible
trials cannot establish wins; at least one warmup and five verified fresh trials
are still required. Only matching input bytes and tracks are compared.

Back up the canonical database and frozen inputs durably before large workloads.
An environment reset can erase both the workspace and temporary database. If
the canonical database is lost, retain session observations as unconfirmed
diagnostics and start a fresh generation; never reconstruct trial records from
logs, summaries, or comparison projections. Re-downloaded Git metadata can
change hashes even at a pinned revision, so re-audit and freeze the new index.

GNU time child peak RSS and the isolated Python worker's peak RSS are
individual process high-water marks. They are not a concurrent process-tree
peak. Process-tree RSS samples are only a lower bound, and sampling is disabled
when namespace PIDs do not match `/proc`. GPU VRAM (GPU competitors only) and
energy stay unknown without reliable instruments. Resource unknowns cannot
establish an efficiency win.

Linux workflow I/O counters are read from `/proc/self/io` before and after the
isolated phase, including waited-for children. Logical read/write bytes and
syscall counts are distinct from storage-layer read bytes, page-dirtying write
bytes, and cancelled write bytes. File-cache state is uncontrolled; these
counters do not establish physical SSD/HDD traffic, latency, or energy. Unknown
physical-device measurements remain unknown. See the kernel's
[I/O accounting documentation](https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/filesystems/proc.html).

Bato `--stats` snapshots report admitted workers, coded blocks, estimated
host memory, the memory budget and the managed peak reservation. They are
engine accounting, not process RSS.

After each retained change, checkpoint its source revision, binary hashes,
commands, run IDs, manifest hashes, correctness/build results, comparison
files, regressions, unmeasured metrics, and next experiment. Favorable averages
do not establish the overall goal: require each declared configuration to win
each workload and metric, and report every missing competitor and loss.

## Diagnostics and next experiment

`examples/cm_probe.rs` reports exact encoded sizes for complete frozen
development files. It supplements the authoritative suite and never
establishes a competitor win. The known gaps are summarized in the README
status; profile the largest measured size gap first, declare each candidate before new trials, and measure fresh
development trials before the full frozen and held-out evaluation.

Selected codecs always receive fresh observations; competitor reuse reads original canonical runs via `--competitor-run`. Historical databases are preserved. A tooling change changes the recorded harness hash: old/new size comparisons remain available when inputs and settings match, but timing and competitor-reference comparisons require identical harness and resource conditions. Never rewrite old identities to bypass that check.

## Correctness tests and competitor measurements

Run the CLI correctness gate with the selected Bato binary.

```bash
python3 scripts/run_tests.py --bato-bin target/release/bato --timeout 180
```

Run competitor measurements as a separate command using a frozen corpus
manifest and the strongest supported profile.

```bash
python3 scripts/benchmark_suite.py --corpus /path/to/corpus \
  --frozen-workloads /path/to/frozen.json --codecs bato,zip,7z \
  --bato-bin target/release/bato --profile high --mode rigorous \
  --warmups 1 --repetitions 5
```

## Bato archive operations

Run this track separately from compressor comparisons. It uses a deterministic
synthetic tree, a same-size edit and a separate add payload. The manifest
records the tree and content identities, operation set and controlled 64 KiB
segment / 16 KiB chunk geometry. Preserve old manifests alongside the
database. Generate a new versioned manifest when changing fixture semantics.

```bash
mkdir -p artifacts/archive-ops
python3 scripts/benchmark_suite.py --archive-operations --list-workloads \
  > artifacts/archive-ops/indexed-v1.json
python3 scripts/benchmark_suite.py --archive-operations \
  --frozen-workloads artifacts/archive-ops/indexed-v1.json \
  --bato-bin target/release/bato --bato-revision HEAD \
  --baseline-bato-bin /path/to/older/bato --baseline-bato-revision OLDER_SHA \
  --bato-modes cpu1,cpu --warmups 1 --repetitions 5 \
  --output-dir artifacts/archive-ops --scratch-dir /var/tmp \
  --checkpoint-dir artifacts/archive-ops-checkpoint
python3 scripts/compare_benchmarks.py --archive-operations \
  --output-dir artifacts/archive-ops --baseline BASELINE_RUN --candidate CANDIDATE_RUN
```

Binary hashes identify measured builds. Revision arguments declare their source
commits. Keep the exact binaries or record how they were built. Use
`--archive-operation-format plain` on both manifest generation and campaign
commands to compare older builds that lack indexed archives. Unsupported
operations are recorded as such. Compare indexed and plain runs separately.

The archive-operation tables share `measurements.sqlite3` with the existing
benchmark schema. Trials are committed individually. A campaign left running
in the database is incomplete. Start a new campaign after interruption.
Preparation uses fresh copies of each build's verified starting archive.
The command runs with an isolated temporary directory. The OS cache is
uncontrolled. Scratch usage is unknown because short-lived files cannot be
reliably measured after each command. The JSON comparison includes raw outcome
labels and per-metric median, minimum, and maximum. Overlapping timing ranges
are uncertain. For a later run on the same host, reuse the recorded worker
and managed-memory settings with `--threads` and `--bato-memory-budget`
if automatic resource detection resolves different values.

The default CLI correctness run (also available as `--min`) includes the standard
rules and ten verified corpus files: the two smallest distinct TXT, PDF, JPG,
PNG, and MP4 inputs. It reads `~/data-research/corpus/.corpus.json`; override
with `--corpus PATH`. Selection sorts by byte size then relative path, checks
sizes and SHA-256 before testing, and rejects missing types or changed inputs.
Each real-file check compresses, tests, restores, and compares exact bytes,
printing original/compressed sizes and elapsed seconds. Controlled fixtures
remain for corruption, malformed data, boundary sizes and recovery. Temporary
outputs are removed and corpus files remain read-only. Before any Bato correctness test, the command registers the selected paths,
hashes and original byte sizes in SQLite, then ensures verified competitor
baselines for ZIP, gzip, 7z, RAR, PAQ8PX, CMIX and NNCP using the benchmark
harness at its high profile, one warmup and five measured repetitions. Only
missing compatible cases are measured; reused observations retain their original
run IDs and dates. File bytes, codec identities, settings, hardware and limits
must match, and committed lossless trials are required. Missing tools, failures
or timeouts stop execution before the Bato tests. This gate never installs tools
or silently lowers settings. Original baselines remain in their source runs;
reference-only campaigns do not copy trials or appear as fresh measurements.
`--database PATH` selects the database (default
`~/data-research/measurements.sqlite3`), `--benchmark-output PATH` selects reports
and scratch (default `~/data-research/test-results`), and
`--competitor-timeout SECONDS` sets the measurement phase timeout (default 600).
The Bato round trips remain correctness checks; their diagnostic elapsed times
do not create new Bato benchmark observations.
`--test NAME` runs only the selected existing rules and needs no corpus.
Explicit `--min` cannot be combined with `--test` or `--extended`; an extended
run also includes the ten real files.

Use `scripts/run_tests.py --extended --timeout 900` for the 100,000-file admission
check, 30,000-file edits, and multi-GiB restore cases. Run these separately from timed campaigns.
# Working on Bato

## Owner priorities (non-negotiable)
1. **Order: compressed size > decompression time > compression time.** The
   higher goal wins every conflict. No size loss for speed; no slower
   decoding for faster encoding.
2. **One mode, always the strongest.** No fast/balanced/selectable modes,
   profiles, or header fields and options that can hold only one value.
3. **Best size and best decompression time** against every tested
   competitor. Target: smaller than paq8px at its strongest setting;
   decompression as fast as possible, ZIP speed as the aspiration. Speed
   work goes to decoding first and never costs size.
4. **Report honestly:** name the competitor, data and margin of every loss.
   Never claim an unmeasured win.

## Repo map
- `src/main.rs` CLI only · `src/lib.rs` public API · `src/core/types.rs`
  format constants, options, errors.
- `src/core/frame.rs` stream framing · `archive.rs` directory catalogue ·
  `codec.rs` memory admission and worker pool · `safe_fs/` anchored output
  and staged extraction · `memory/` budgets and platform probes.
- `src/core/cm/` model: `predictor.rs` ensemble, `models/` experts
  (order, word, match, record, neighbours), `mixer.rs`, `apm.rs`,
  `isse.rs`, `simd.rs` kernels, `segment.rs` chunk coding, `x86.rs`
  transform.
- `docs/format.md` wire format · `docs/benchmark-iterations.md` measurement
  procedure · `scripts/` benchmark tooling and its tests.

## Engine
- Pure Rust; never call another compressor inside Bato (competitors and tar
  only in benchmark tooling).
- No proprietary code or algorithms: nothing from closed-source, patented,
  licence-restricted or reverse-engineered compressors (e.g. RAR, KGB
  internals), and no code whose licence is incompatible with
  `MIT OR Apache-2.0` (e.g. copying GPL paq8px/cmix source). Use only ideas
  from public, unencumbered descriptions (papers, books, public-domain
  specifications), implement them originally, and name the source in the
  module's `Provenance:` header. Dependencies must be permissively licensed.
- Universal byte archiver: accept any regular-file contents (empty, invalid
  UTF-8, compressed, encrypted, unknown formats). Never choose a codec, or
  reject input, by name, extension, MIME type or signature. Decisions follow
  byte patterns and bounded costs; transforms keep the raw fallback and
  count their metadata and CPU in selection.
- Streams and directory archives are told apart by the header flag, never a
  suffix. Default output names keep the complete source name. No silent
  filename conversion; portable path restrictions live in `docs/format.md`.
- Incompressible data is stored raw with bounded overhead; never claim every
  input shrinks.
- Bit-identical output on every platform and thread count: integer only
  (enforced by `cm_sources_are_integer_only`), no RNG, clocks, floats,
  hash-map order or scheduling-dependent results. Pseudo-random values come
  only from fixed-seed integer hashes.
- Keep checksums, bounded memory and output, path, symlink and device-file
  safety, and staged extraction.
- Multicore: independent segments run on all admitted workers. No GPU path
  unless it is measured to improve size or decoding with identical bytes.
- Development mode: no backward compatibility. Break the format freely, bump
  the version in `src/core/types.rs`, update `docs/format.md`, and reject
  unsupported data clearly. Delete legacy decoders and compatibility tests.

## Resources
- Default runs use `--threads 0 --memory-budget auto` (all CPUs, 90% of
  usable RAM, 10% headroom). Benchmarks resolve and record these once;
  `--resume` keeps them. Explicit limits are only for labelled controlled
  tests.
- Competitors get identical CPU and process limits, and every run needs
  enough independent work for its workers. Segment geometry changes are a
  different configuration; report them, never substitute them silently.

## Workflow
1. Read the code and `docs/format.md` first. Find the largest measured gap,
   profile it, and make one focused change.
2. Iterate on the frozen development manifest (Canterbury; Calgary is
   held out; see `docs/benchmark-iterations.md`). Pilot files of at most
   2 MiB each and at most 8 MiB in total, whole files only.
3. Pass the gates below, then benchmark the candidate against the previous
   best on identical hardware and settings.
4. Keep a change only for a measured useful gain: compare the exact encoded
   bytes and the extra compression and decompression CPU, and record the
   regressions. A pure refactor must keep archives byte-identical.
5. Commit and push each retained, verified improvement to `main` before the
   next experiment, against the expected current `main` SHA, preserving
   concurrent changes and file modes. Never force-push. Record source and
   binary identities and accepted run IDs in the ignored checkpoint.
   Rejected experiments stay off `main`: no feature flags or options for
   inferior variants.
6. Full evaluation only after the gates pass: frozen and held-out
   manifests, `--mode rigorous --warmups 1 --repetitions 5` (large cases via
   `--large-warmups`/`--large-repetitions`). First compare the manifest's
   paths, file counts and bytes with the intended set; set directory caps
   explicitly; label reduced coverage as partial.
7. If blocked by the environment: checkpoint the source and binary
   identities, commands, results and the next step; say the goal is
   incomplete. Don't loop on setup.

### Gates
```bash
cargo fmt --check && cargo clippy --release --all-targets
cargo test --release
python3 -m unittest discover -s scripts/tests
python3 scripts/run_tests.py --bato-bin target/release/bato --timeout 180
```
Scalar/SIMD parity tests are part of `cargo test`. Correctness fixtures
cover misleading names, all byte values, empty and duplicate files, nested
compressed data and mixed trees, checking exact bytes and structure.
For a refactor, also build the previous `main` as a reference, compress the
development files with both binaries and `cmp` every archive.

## Benchmarking
- Use only `scripts/download_corpus.py`, `scripts/benchmark_suite.py` and
  `scripts/compare_benchmarks.py`; extend them rather than adding parallel
  tools. Don't re-download verified data.
- Freeze dataset identities, hashes, roles and settings before evaluating:
  `--list-workloads > …frozen.json`, then `--frozen-workloads`. Duplicates
  of development data are not independent held-out evidence.
- Competitors (paq8px, paq8l, zpaq, cmix, nncp, KGB, 7z, ZIP, RAR, xz, zstd,
  gzip, LZ4, bzip2 where available): preflight with `--check-tools`, record
  versions, and use the strongest supported profile (`--profile high`;
  paq8px runs `-12L -lstmlayers=5`). Record resource-limited PAQ settings
  explicitly; never substitute a lower level silently. GPU competitors
  (`--nncp-cuda`) are a separate hardware track.
- Run each competitor once per workload, profile, track, hardware and limits
  as a baseline campaign; then reuse it with `compare_benchmarks.py
  --competitor-run` while only Bato changes, reporting its run ID and date.
  Never copy stored observations into new trials or present them as fresh.
- During development run `benchmark_suite.py --codecs bato` only.
- Verify every restored file and directory; delete temporary archives and
  extraction trees but keep their measurements. Unavailable tools, failures,
  timeouts and zero-exit partial archives are failures, not Bato wins
  (`--allow-unavailable` records them as such).
- Record size and ratio, compression and decompression wall and CPU time,
  throughput, process-tree RAM, hardware, failures and uncertainty. VRAM,
  energy and I/O only where instruments are reliable; otherwise "unknown".
- Keep single-worker and all-worker runs (`--bato-modes cpu1,cpu`), native
  and common-tar tracks separate; every setting goes in the run identity.
  The tar track packages exactly the frozen members, revalidates after
  packaging, rejects changed native inventories and keeps hidden files.
  Nothing else may run during timed trials.
- Report wins, ties, losses and uncertain results per workload and metric,
  for one declared Bato configuration; never combine the best metrics from
  different runs.
- Datasets, reports, databases, binaries and checkpoints live in ignored
  directories. Keep live scratch (`--scratch-dir`) outside synced folders;
  mirror evidence with `--checkpoint-dir`.

## Code quality (DRY, KISS, YAGNI)
- One implementation per rule, constant and format detail; the encoder and
  decoder share one model path (`Predictor::probability`/`observe`). Prefer
  plain functions and data to single-use traits and generics. Build nothing
  speculative.
- Delete dead and commented-out code; no `#[allow(dead_code)]` and no unused
  parameters. Test-only code is `#[cfg(test)]`.
- Modules have one responsibility and stay under about 800 lines.
- At most four parameters (otherwise use a struct); no boolean flag
  parameters (use an enum); enums and newtypes instead of bare integers and
  strings.
- Named `const`s with unit and reason: format constants in `types.rs` or the
  owning module, model tuning at the top of its model.
- Nesting at most three levels; return early.
- No mutable globals (only exception: the test heap observer in
  `src/core/diagnostics.rs`). No `dyn Any` control flow. No
  `unwrap`/`expect` on input-dependent library paths.
- Every `unsafe` block has a `// SAFETY:` comment. SIMD and pointer code also
  needs a test against the scalar or safe reference.
- Never discard errors silently (`let _ =`, `.ok()`, `unwrap_or_default()`)
  without a comment on that line saying why.
- Methods that mutate say so in their name or docs. Getters, predicates,
  `Display` and `Drop` do no I/O and no unrelated mutation.
- `src/main.rs` only parses arguments, calls the library and prints.
- Fix violations in code you touch; record larger ones as follow-up work.
# Bato Requirements & Dependencies

This document outlines all requirements and dependencies for developing, building, testing, and benchmarking the Bato compression engine.

---

## 1. Rust Compiler & Toolchain (Bato Engine)

Bato's compression engine is implemented in pure Rust (2021 edition) with no external compressor dependencies.

| Component | Minimum Version | Purpose |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **rustc** | `1.89+` (see `rust-version` in `Cargo.toml`) | Rust compiler |
| **cargo** | `1.89+` | Build manager & package manager |
| **rustup** | Latest | Toolchain installer & target manager |
| **musl target** *(optional)* | `x86_64-unknown-linux-musl` | Static, portable CPU-only builds |
| **musl-tools** *(optional)* | Latest | C toolchain for musl target linking |

### Installation

```bash
# Install Rust toolchain
curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh

# Optional: Add musl target for portable CPU builds
rustup target add x86_64-unknown-linux-musl
sudo apt-get install -y musl-tools
```

---

## 2. Python Environment (Scripts & Benchmarks)

Bato's benchmarking, corpus management, and verification scripts live in `scripts/`. They are strictly designed to run on the standard library with zero mandatory third-party packages.

| Component | Minimum Version | Purpose |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **python3** | `3.8+` (tested with `3.14`) | Runs benchmark runner, corpus downloader, and verification |
| **Standard Library** | Built-in | `argparse`, `json`, `sqlite3`, `pathlib`, `struct`, `urllib`, `tarfile`, `zipfile`, `unittest`, `dataclasses` |
| **pytest / ruff** *(optional)* | Latest | Optional testing and linting tools |

---

## 3. External Archiver Competitors (Benchmark Suite)

Bato's evaluation suite compares Bato against industry-standard compressors under identical execution profiles (`fast`, `default`, `high` / `extreme`).

### Standard Package Competitors

These can be installed directly through system package managers:

| Codec | Binary / Command | Debian / Ubuntu Package | Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **7-Zip** | `7zz` or `7z` | `p7zip-full` | High profile uses `-mx=9` |
| **ZIP** | `zip` & `unzip` | `zip`, `unzip` | Native info-zip (Python fallback if absent) |
| **Gzip** | `gzip` | `gzip` | Standard DEFLATE `-9` |
| **Bzip2** | `bzip2` | `bzip2` | Burrows-Wheeler `-9` (Python fallback if absent) |
| **XZ** | `xz` | `xz-utils` | LZMA2 `--extreme -9` |
| **Zstandard** | `zstd` | `zstd` | `--ultra -22` |
| **LZ4** | `lz4` | `lz4` | `-12` |
| **Tar** | `tar` | `tar` | Used for common-tar comparison tracks |
| **ZPAQ** | `zpaq` | `zpaq` | Journaling archiver `-method 5` |

### Specialized Competitors

| Codec | Binary | Source / Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **RAR** | `rar` | Official CLI from [RARLAB](https://www.rarlab.com/download.htm) (`unrar` alone cannot compress) |
| **KGB** | `kgb` | KGB Archiver v1.0 CLI (PAQ-based) |
| **PAQ8L** | `paq8l` | Matt Mahoney PAQ8L executable placed on `PATH` |
| **PAQ8PX** | `paq8px` | Jan Ondrus PAQ8PX executable placed on `PATH` |

---

## 4. Verification

You can verify which tools are present and ready on your system at any time by running:

```bash
# Check status of all benchmark competitors
python3 scripts/benchmark_suite.py --check-tools --allow-unavailable

# Run Bato's Rust and Python test suite
cargo test
python3 -m unittest discover -s scripts/tests -p 'test_*.py'
```

---

## 5. Quick Setup for Debian / Ubuntu

```bash
# 1. System packages & standard archivers
sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y \
    build-essential \
    musl-tools \
    python3 \
    p7zip-full \
    zip \
    unzip \
    gzip \
    bzip2 \
    xz-utils \
    zstd \
    lz4 \
    tar \
    zpaq \
    kgb

# 2. Rust toolchain
curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh
rustup target add x86_64-unknown-linux-musl
```


### Optional extreme neural/context-mixing competitors

- **CMIX**: build [byronknoll/cmix](https://github.com/byronknoll/cmix), executable `cmix` on PATH; at least 32 GiB RAM recommended. Single-file input and no common `-9` quality switch.
- **NNCP**: obtain [Bellard NNCP v3.3](https://bellard.org/nncp/), executable `nncp` on PATH; the benchmark uses CPU and `--profile default` unless explicitly configured. For supported NVIDIA GPUs use `--nncp-cuda`; the `enwik8/enwik9` profiles are text-specific.
- **PAQ8PX and ZPAQ** were already supported; PAQ8PX high can exceed 32 GiB. All four must pass independent capability round-trip checks before results are accepted.

`python3 scripts/benchmark_suite.py --check-tools --codecs cmix,nncp,paq8px,zpaq --tool-check-timeout 600 --allow-unavailable`

Use `--memory-budget` for the process address-space cap, `--timeout` for each measured phase, and `--nncp-profile` to explicitly freeze NNCP model choice. Missing GPU, peak process RAM, or timing data remain unknown, not performance wins.
