Abstract. We study the effect of temperature on the growth rate of a common soil bacterium under laboratory conditions. Cultures were grown at twelve temperatures between 5 and 45 degrees Celsius, and the optical density was measured every thirty minutes for three days. The growth rate increased roughly linearly up to 32 degrees, reached a maximum at 37 degrees, and fell sharply above 40 degrees. These results are consistent with earlier work on related species and suggest that the enzyme responsible for cell wall synthesis is the limiting factor at high temperature. Further experiments are needed to test this hypothesis.

