Marie Curie was born in Warsaw in 1867 and moved to Paris in 1891 to study physics and mathematics. Together with her husband Pierre she discovered the elements polonium and radium, and in 1903 the two shared the Nobel Prize in Physics with Henri Becquerel. After her husband's death in 1906 she took over his professorship at the Sorbonne, becoming the first woman to teach there. In 1911 she received a second Nobel Prize, this time in Chemistry. She died in 1934 of a disease caused by long exposure to radiation.

