The first law of thermodynamics states that energy can neither be created nor destroyed, only converted from one form to another. The second law states that the entropy of an isolated system never decreases over time. Together, these laws explain why heat flows from hot objects to cold ones, why engines can never be perfectly efficient, and why perpetual motion machines are impossible. The unit of energy in the International System is the joule, and the unit of temperature is the kelvin.

