Photosynthesis is the process by which green plants, algae and some bacteria convert light energy into chemical energy. In the leaves of a plant, chlorophyll absorbs sunlight and uses it to turn carbon dioxide from the air and water from the soil into glucose and oxygen. The glucose is used to build the cellulose of the cell walls and to store energy as starch, while the oxygen is released into the atmosphere. Without this process there would be very little oxygen to breathe, and almost every food chain on the planet would collapse.

