Computers store information as sequences of bits. A single bit can be either zero or one, and eight bits make a byte, which can represent any number from zero to two hundred and fifty five. Text is stored by assigning a number to each character, so that the letter A is sixty five and the letter a is ninety seven. Images are stored as grids of colored points, and sound is stored as a long list of numbers that describe how the air pressure changes over time. Compression works by finding patterns in these numbers and describing them in a shorter way, so that the same information takes up less space.

